ll integer points (x,y) with x,y ≥ 1 and x+y ≤ k+1? The solution then shows that for even k ≥ 2, impossible; for odd k, only k=1 or k=3 possible.\n\nWe need to verify that reasoning is correct.\n\nFirst, check the reduction: The claim that we can replace each non-sunny line by a vertical line that passes through the leftmost column not yet occupied by a vertical line, and that this does not reduce coverage. Let's examine: Non-sunny lines are horizontal (slope 0), vertical (slope ∞), or slope -1. Horizontal lines cover at most one point per column (since they have constant y). Slope -1 lines also cover at most one point per column (since x+y constant). Vertical lines cover all points in a column. So if we have a non-sunny line that is not vertical, we can replace it by a vertical line at a column that is not yet covered by a vertical line. Since vertical lines cover all points in that column, they will cover at least as many points as the original line (which covered at most one point per column). However, we need to ensure that the new vertical line does not conflict with other lines (they must be distinct). Since we can choose a column not yet used by a vertical line, we can keep distinctness. So the reduction seems valid.\n\nThus we can assume non-sunny lines are vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-k. This is a standard reduction.\n\nNow we need to cover T_k with k sunny lines. The solution then provides a bound: each sunny line can contain at most floor((k+1)/2) points of T_k. They derive this bound using the representation of a line as p x - q y = c, with gcd(p,q)=1, q>0, p≠0, p≠ -q (to avoid slopes 0, ∞, -1). Then integer points on the line are (x0+qt, y0+pt). The sum x+y = (x0+y0)+(p+q)t. Since p+q ≠ 0 (sunny condition), the sum changes by at least 1 per step. The sum must stay between 2 and k+1 (since points in T_k have x+y between 2 and k+1). So the number of integer t values is at most floor((k+1-2)/|p+q|)+1 = floor((k-1)/|p+q|)+1 ≤ floor((k-1)/2)+1 = floor((k+1)/2)