t we could use horizontal or diagonal lines to cover points across columns. However, the structure may limit the ability to cover all points with only n-4 non-sunny lines plus k sunny lines.\n\nThe solution's argument for k≥4 is too simplistic: \"sunny lines cover at most one point per column.\" But we also have non-sunny lines that can cover many points per column. So we need to see if the combination can cover all points. The solution may rely on a more detailed counting argument: For each column, the number of points is decreasing: column i has n+1-i points. The total number of points is n(n+1)/2. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so total points covered by sunny lines is at most k * n (since there are n columns). The non-sunny lines can cover many points, but each non-sunny line can cover at most n points? Let's see: a vertical line can cover at most n points (the column with n points). A horizontal line can cover at most n points (the row with n points). A diagonal line x+y=c can cover at most n points (the diagonal with n points). So each non-sunny line can cover at most n points. So total points covered by non-sunny lines is at most (n-k) * n. So total points covered by all lines is at most k * n + (n-k) * n = n * n = n^2. But the total number of points is n(n+1)/2, which is less than n^2 for n≥2. So this bound is not restrictive. So that doesn't help.\n\nWe need a more refined argument: The non-sunny lines are limited in type: vertical lines cover entire columns, but there are n columns, and we have at most n-k non-sunny lines. So we can cover at most n-k columns fully with vertical lines. The remaining columns must be covered by sunny lines (or horizontal/diagonal lines). But horizontal lines cover rows, which intersect many columns. However, each horizontal line can cover at most one point per column (since it's horizontal, it intersects each column at exactly one point). So horizontal lines also cover at most one point per column. Similarly, diagonal lines also intersect each column at exactly one point (since x+y=c => y = c - x, so for each x there is exactly one y). So any non-vertical non-sunny line also covers at most one point per column. So the only type of non-sunny line that can cover more than one point per column is vertical lines. So the only way to cover many points in a column is to use a vertical line. So the argument may be: For each column, the number of points that can be covered by non-vertical lines (horizontal or diagonal) is at most one per column per line. So the total number of points covered in column i by non-vertical lines is at most (n-k) (since there are at most n-k non-vertical lines). But we also have vertical lines that can cover many points in a column. So we need to consider the number of vertical lines we can have. Since we have at most n-k non-sunny lines total, the number of vertical lines is at most n-k. So we can have at most n-k columns fully covered by vertical lines. The remaining columns must be covered by sunny lines (or horizontal/diagonal lines) each covering at most one point per column. So for each remaining column, we need at least as many sunny lines as the number of points in that column (since each sunny line can cover at most one point per column). But we have only k sunny lines. So we need to check if there exists a column with more points than k among the columns not covered by vertical lines. Since we have at most n-k vertical lines, we can cover at most n-k columns fully. There are n columns total. So there are at least k columns not covered by vertical lines (since n - (n-k) = k). Actually, if we use all n-k non-sunny lines as vertical lines, we cover n-k columns fully. The remaining k columns are not fully covered by vertical lines. In each of those columns, we need to cover all points using sunny lines (or horizontal/diagonal lines). But each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so we need at least as many sunny lines as t